"""Original developmentally appropriate child/family support planning."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-059","Child Adolescent and Family Psychosocial Support","child-adolescent-and-family-psychosocial-support","Child Adolescent and\nFamily Psychosocial\nSupport",
 "Prepare a fictional child/family support outline that considers development, participation, caregiver safety and intersectoral access without diagnosing, treating or deciding legal consent.",[
m("Development participation and everyday settings","Identify developmentally appropriate support questions without turning an observation into a diagnosis.",[
"Children and adolescents differ in development, communication, interests, relationships and dependence on others. A support discussion should consider these differences alongside disability, language and the young person's own priorities. A change in school participation or a report of distress is a reason for an appropriate response, not a diagnosis by itself. WHO and UNICEF's service guidance emphasises rights and developmental fit rather than one service model for every setting. An introductory learner can identify relevant questions and qualified roles; assessing a mental-health condition or choosing treatment requires appropriate professional competence. [S23, S1]",
"Everyday settings such as home, school, community activities and health services can influence access to support. Ask what practical conditions make participation easier or harder without assigning blame to a child or family. The child's view and the caregiver's account may differ, and neither should be silently treated as the only perspective. Qualified staff need appropriate age- and communication-sensitive ways of hearing the young person. Coursework uses supplied fictional descriptions only: do not interview children, seek real disclosures, administer a screening tool or ask someone to reproduce a distressing experience for marks. [S23, S1]",
"Participation means explaining the purpose of an encounter and giving a young person an appropriate opportunity to express preferences. It does not mean promising every requested outcome or assuming participation alone authorises all information sharing. Consent, assent, caregiver involvement and legal duties are decision- and context-specific. This course provides no universal age rule, diagnosis threshold or reporting protocol. Where an actual concern arises, use designated qualified child-safeguarding, clinical and emergency procedures rather than improvise from a fictional exercise or allow a routine service discussion to delay an appropriate urgent response. [S23, S1]",
"A useful introductory note separates an observation, its context, the child's stated priority and an unanswered question. Describe an access need, such as an understandable explanation or an accessible contact arrangement, without assuming a particular clinical cause. Identify who can take the next appropriate step and what needs qualified review. Use non-stigmatising language and avoid recording unnecessary identifying details. The learning task is to prepare a bounded support question and responsibility map, not to label a child, determine parental fault or demonstrate independent child mental-health practice through a short worksheet. [S23, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an adolescent stops attending an after-school club because its changed timetable conflicts with caring responsibilities. Adults call this a mental-health diagnosis without hearing the adolescent's account. The learner separates the observed absence, practical context and appropriate support questions.",
"Write a note with the observation, a contextual question, the young person's priority, an access adjustment and a qualified decision boundary. Avoid a diagnosis or judgement about the family.",
"A defensible note considers development and context, hears the young person's perspective appropriately and separates a participation change from a clinical conclusion.",["S23","S1"],[
("A participation change does not establish a diagnosis.","A fictional adolescent misses a club after its timetable changes. What should a learner do first within this exercise?","Separate the observation from possible context and appropriate support questions.",["Diagnose a disorder from absence alone.","Assume all non-attendance has one clinical cause.","Label the family responsible without further information."],"The observation needs contextual interpretation rather than an unsupported diagnosis."),
("Development and communication affect participation.","A support service uses identical explanations for every age and communication need. What should be reviewed?","Whether explanations and participation opportunities fit the young person's development and access needs.",["Only whether the form has a logo.","A rule that all ages understand options identically.","A requirement that children disclose distress publicly."],"Developmentally appropriate support includes accessible communication."),
("Child and caregiver perspectives may differ.","A fictional caregiver's account differs from the adolescent's stated concern. What should a support outline avoid?","Automatically treating one account as the complete perspective without qualified consideration.",["Retaining the difference as information to consider.","Using appropriate safeguarding procedures.","Identifying communication needs."],"Different accounts should remain visible within a suitable qualified process."),
("Participation is not blanket authorisation.","A fictional young person agrees to discuss an activity. What should not be assumed?","Permission for every disclosure, referral or legal decision.",["That an accessible explanation can help.","That the young person's views matter.","That qualified local procedures remain necessary."],"Consent, assent and information permissions require decision-specific handling."),
("Coursework should not recruit real child cases.","A facilitator wants learners to interview children about distress as an assessment task. What fits this course instead?","Analyse the supplied fictional descriptions without real interviews or screening.",["Use the youngest available child as practice.","Collect identifying school and health records.","Administer an unapproved clinical scale for marks."],"Introductory learning does not authorise real child assessment or sensitive collection."),
]),
m("Caregiver involvement and family support","Plan bounded caregiver engagement that considers the young person's views, safety and evidence limits.",[
"Families and caregivers can provide practical, emotional and relational support, but involvement should not be assumed safe, desired or appropriate in every circumstance. A qualified process considers the young person's views, development, relevant law, privacy, potential conflict and safeguarding concerns. The fact that an adult accompanies a child does not settle all these questions. An introductory learner can identify the need for that process and communication support without deciding a real consent exception or arranging an unsupervised family meeting. Explain role limits honestly rather than promise absolute confidentiality or a specific outcome. [S23, S1]",
"A family-support outline should be specific about the goal and the kind of involvement proposed. Help with transport, a discussion about scheduling and a clinical family intervention are different activities with different responsibilities. Clarify what information is necessary for each purpose and who is authorised to share it. Do not send a full health account to every adult or school contact because they may be helpful. Where a caregiver has support needs, identify an appropriate route without turning the child into the caregiver's worker or making the child's assistance conditional on caregiver participation. [S23, S1]",
"The 2026 parenting review synthesises voluntary programmes for parents of adolescents aged eleven to twenty-one, using studies with face-to-face components. Its last reported search is December 2023. Diverse outcomes and methods prevented meta-analysis, and the review has screening and registration limitations. It can inform questions about accessible settings, facilitator preparation and engagement; it does not supply a universal family treatment, justify mandated participation or establish effects for every younger child. A recent publication date is not permission to copy a programme or extend its findings to unstudied groups and delivery formats. [R31, S23]",
"For the fictional exercise, identify the young person's goal, a possible supportive role, an uncertainty about safety or permission and the qualified decision route. Retain a way to revisit preferences and practical barriers. A family can face time, income, language or transport constraints without being unwilling to help, so avoid blame from a missed session. Any actual concern about violence, neglect or urgent risk belongs within designated qualified safeguarding and clinical procedures. The course assesses careful planning and evidence interpretation, not a learner's ability to conduct family therapy or settle a disputed care decision. [S23, S1, R31]"],
"Fictional case: a service offers a fixed-time caregiver session and treats non-attendance as a lack of concern, although the caregiver works at that time. The adolescent wants help with school access but has not agreed to broad sharing. The learner separates practical support, permissions and qualified decisions.",
"Draft a caregiver-engagement outline with the young person's goal, the proposed role, scheduling/access question, purpose-specific information boundary, safety uncertainty and qualified follow-up owner.",
"A sound outline treats caregiver involvement as a considered support option. It recognises access barriers and the limits of the parenting evidence without imposing participation, sharing broadly or promising treatment effects.",["S23","S1","R31"],[
("Caregiver involvement needs context-specific consideration.","A fictional service assumes any accompanying adult may receive a child's full account. What needs review?","The adult's role, safety, relevant permissions and qualified safeguarding process.",["Only whether the adult arrived first.","A rule that accompaniment grants all authority.","A belief that privacy does not apply to young people."],"Presence alone does not determine safe or authorised involvement."),
("Practical family support differs from clinical family intervention.","A caregiver helps organise transport to an appointment. What should not be inferred?","That the caregiver has delivered or is qualified to deliver family therapy.",["That transport can affect access.","That role clarity is useful.","That the young person's priorities remain relevant."],"Different activities have different purposes and competency requirements."),
("The parenting review has population and delivery limits.","A learner applies the 2026 review to prove a mandated digital-only programme for all young children works. What is the problem?","The review concerns voluntary adolescent-parent programmes with face-to-face components and cannot justify that transfer.",["A recent issue date validates every population.","Every parenting programme is the same intervention.","Narrative synthesis removes all applicability limits."],"Age, voluntary participation and delivery format constrain interpretation."),
("Missed sessions can reflect access constraints.","A fictional caregiver misses a meeting that conflicts with paid work. What should be considered?","The timing barrier and feasible engagement options rather than an automatic motivation judgement.",["Proof that the caregiver does not care.","A diagnosis from the attendance record.","A requirement to disclose more private information as a penalty."],"Attendance is affected by practical conditions as well as preferences."),
("Proposed help does not authorise all information sharing.","A school contact may help with access and requests the entire health history. What should a plan clarify?","The specific purpose, necessary information and appropriate permissions/qualified authority.",["Automatic full disclosure to anyone offering help.","A copy in the class worksheet.","A rule that intersectoral support cancels privacy."],"Support coordination still requires proportionate authorised information handling."),
]),
m("School community pathways and transitions","Specify a feasible intersectoral support pathway with qualified ownership and an appropriate review question.",[
"A child or adolescent may need support from education, health, social services and community organisations at the same time. WHO and UNICEF describe coordinated service networks while recognising that no single model fits all settings. Map the specific need, the role that can respond and the practical link between services rather than list every possible agency. A school can consider an access adjustment within its authority; clinical assessment and treatment remain with qualified professionals. Do not promise a specialist place or infer eligibility from an international service example. [S23, S21, S1]",
"Transitions can create gaps when age eligibility, contact roles, school routines or service locations change. Clarify who holds responsibility during the change, what the receiving service has accepted and how the young person and appropriate supporters understand the next step. Sending a referral does not establish continuity. Current service information, safe contact arrangements and appropriate information permissions are needed before a real handover. A fictional plan can identify those dependencies without making a call on behalf of a real child, transferring records or inventing an emergency number or waiting-time guarantee. [S23, S21]",
"The policy introduction links service quality with workforce, governance and social conditions. The parenting review raises implementation questions about facilitator preparation, engagement and setting, with limited transfer beyond its studied programmes. Together they encourage an honest readiness check: accepted roles, appropriate staff, access resources and a feedback process. A diagram of a pathway is an output rather than evidence that children can use it. If a service gap remains unresolved, state the gap and responsible decision route instead of recording support as completed or assigning responsibility to the young person. [S22, R31, S23]",
"A useful review question concerns whether a specified support became accessible and met the young person's agreed goal, within approved ethical arrangements. It is different from claiming that symptoms improved or that a programme caused recovery. Examine missing perspectives, changing circumstances and possible burdens on children or caregivers. Coursework uses only fictional material and invented observations, with no real outcome survey or copied clinical instrument. An accountable plan names the role that will respond to the finding, the review point and any qualified decision still needed. Curriculum approval and supervised skills assessment remain separate requirements. [S1, S23, S21]"],
"Fictional case: an adolescent is about to leave a youth service's age range. A referral email is marked complete before the receiving service confirms eligibility or a first contact. The learner identifies the continuity gap and a responsible handover/review route.",
"Prepare a transition map with the goal, sending/receiving roles, acceptance and access dependencies, information boundary, fallback decision route and a bounded review question.",
"A defensible map shows actual responsibility and unresolved dependencies. It distinguishes a referral sent from support accessed and does not turn an administrative transfer into a claim of clinical recovery.",["S23","S21","S1","S22","R31"],[
("A network map needs specific accepted roles.","A fictional pathway lists many agencies but no one owns the next step. What should be added?","The relevant need, responsible role and accepted action between services.",["More agency logos without responsibilities.","A promise that every listed agency has capacity.","An assumption that a diagram itself delivers care."],"Coordination requires clear actions and responsibility rather than a service list."),
("A referral sent does not establish continuity.","An adolescent's transfer is marked complete before receiving-service acceptance. What is still unresolved?","Eligibility, accepted responsibility and a feasible next contact within appropriate arrangements.",["Only whether the email was formatted well.","A guaranteed clinical outcome.","A rule that no one remains responsible after sending."],"Administrative transmission is not completed access or continuity."),
("International service examples do not prove local availability.","A learner promises a local specialist place because WHO describes an example elsewhere. What correction is needed?","Verify actual local scope and capacity through authorised roles before making an availability claim.",["Use the example's waiting time as a universal guarantee.","Assume all systems have identical eligibility.","Treat a source citation as a booking confirmation."],"Examples inform comparison rather than establish current local service status."),
("Readiness includes staff and access resources.","A fictional pathway has agreed arrows but no trained staff or access arrangements. How should it be described?","Conditional on resolving the staffing and access dependencies.",["Ready solely because the map is complete.","Approved because the title includes psychosocial support.","A substitute for independent safeguarding review."],"Implementation needs conditions beyond an administrative design."),
("An access review is not a clinical recovery claim.","A fictional handover review confirms the next appointment was accessible. What remains a separate question?","Clinical outcomes and any causal programme-effect claim.",["Whether the referral action was documented.","Whether access was the stated review question.","Whether a responsible review role was named."],"Evidence of access supports a limited claim, not all health outcomes."),
])])
